CM_ "Renault 5 E-Tech (CMF-BEV) reverse-engineering sketch, rev 4 (2026-04-22).
Two physical buses observed in the 2026-04-19 cabana session:
  bus 0 - 'HS-CAN / powertrain+chassis FD', ~64 addresses
  bus 1 - 'ITS2-FD / body+ADAS FD',         ~113 addresses
Renault convention (observed across every periodic message with a counter):
  - 4-bit message counter in the HIGH nibble of byte 5 (bits 47..44)
  - checksum byte (8-bit) immediately before the counter byte

CHECKSUM ALGORITHM: CRACKED (2026-04-22). Identical to GWM Haval H6:
  CRC-8 polynomial 0x1D, init 0, XOR-output with per-message constant.
  Data covers 7 bytes (matches GWM's dat[N+1:N+8] convention).
```
def renault_checksum(data_7_bytes, xor_out):
    crc = 0
    poly = 0x1D
    for byte in data_7_bytes:
        crc ^= byte
        for _ in range(8):
            crc = ((crc << 1) ^ poly) if (crc & 0x80) else (crc << 1)
            crc &= 0xFF
    return crc ^ xor_out
```
Validated at 100.0000% match across 19M+ frames / 173 segments / 20 active
drives on 20 of 21 messages. Only 0x546 shows 99.93% (11 corrupted frames
out of 15203 - likely transmission errors at drive boundaries). Six of the
26 recorded sessions were EMPTY (panda connection failures).

XOR constants per (msg, subframe_index):
  0x08C A=0xF5 | 0x12F A=0x74 | 0x17C A=0x52 | 0x44C A=0x08
  0x453 A=0xD3 | 0x5CF A=0xEB
  0x1AB A=0x6A B=0x17 C=0xCC  (3-subframe, checksums at bytes 4/14/26)
  0x226 A=0x97 B=0x08 C=0x76  (3-subframe, checksums at bytes 4/23/35)
  0x112 A=0x7E B=0xA2 C=0x2A D=0xC7  (4-subframe, checksums at 4/13/23/33)
  0x455 A=0x0C | 0x534 A=0x47 | 0x5E4 A=0x3C
  0x434 A=0x22 | 0x546 A=0x2D
Failed to match (likely non-standard or no CRC): 0x193, 0x21C, 0x25F,
  0x298, 0x3AC, 0x3E6, 0x422, 0x666 - these "checksums" at byte 4 are
  probably something else (rolling codes or data).

Note: 0x226 subframe B/C have the checksum byte AFTER the data bytes (not
before), unlike the typical GWM convention. This still works with the same
algorithm - just different byte ordering.
Multi-subframe CAN-FD frames repeat that (CHECKSUM, COUNTER) pattern every
10 or 16 bytes; see 0x112 (4 subframes), 0x1AB (3 subframes), 0x226 (3 subframes).
Address-on-both-buses conflicts: 0x534 is 12B on bus 0 vs 24B on bus 1 -
this file describes the bus 1 variant.

Intentional signal overlaps (present to allow both byte-level and bit-level
views of the same data; not a decoding error):
  - 0x134.BYPASSME_SECOC_MAC covers bytes 16-17 including byte 17 high nibble
    where COUNTER lives (SecOC MAC is computed OVER the counter, so they share).
  - 0x58B.TURN_SIGNAL_BYTE11 is the full byte-level view of byte 11; bit 1 is
    extracted separately as TURN_SIGNAL_RIGHT.
  - 0x588.STATUS_B_BYTE19 is the byte-level view; bit 1 is TURN_SIGNAL_RIGHT_ECHO.
  - 0x302.OBJ_B_BYTE37_2 and OBJ_B_BYTE37 are two separate sub-fields of byte 37.

== Signals needed for openpilot lateral control (status) ==
FOUND:
  - STEER_ANGLE       = 0x1AB.STEER_ANGLE     (bus 0, 100Hz, 0.1 deg; also 0x112.STEER_ANGLE_1/2)
  - STEER_RATE        = 0x1AB.STEER_RATE      (bus 0, 100Hz, 0.01 deg/s; also 0x112 8-bit)
  - DRIVER_TORQUE     = 0x17C.DRIVER_TORQUE   (bus 0, 100Hz, 16-bit biased-unsigned, zero-centered, Nissan-style)
  - LKAS_TORQUE_CMD   = 0x1AB byte 7          (8-bit with offset -150, zero-centered)
  - LKAS_STATE        = 0x1AB byte 9 bits 1-2 (2-bit enum: 1=active/ready, 2=suppressed-by-driver, 3=unavailable)
  - VEHICLE_SPEED     = 0x3FA.VEHICLE_SPEED_HIGH_RES (13-bit) + VEHICLE_SPEED_KPH (8-bit)
                        NOTE: 0x226.UNK_SPEED_LIKE_A/B do NOT cleanly decode as km/h
                        (29.2 baseline at rest, 1.5x ratio vs actual speed).
  - WHEEL_SPEED_*     = 0x226.WHEEL_SPEED_{FL,FR,RL,RR}
  - GEAR              = 0x12F.GEAR_STATE {1=R, 2=N, 3=B, 8=D} + PARK_ACTIVE
                        Plus redundant 0x44C.GEAR_DIRECTION (2-bit enum: REVERSE/STATIONARY_PN/FORWARD_DB)
                        Plus 0x453.REVERSE_LIGHT (1-bit, R-only)
                        Plus 0x224.GEAR_SELECTED (not-neutral flag)
                        Plus 0x112.FORWARD_GEAR_ACTIVE (D or B active)
    All 5 gears validated across 149 segments + dedicated gear-test drive.
  - ACCEL_PEDAL       = 0x08C.ACCEL_PEDAL     (bus 0, 100Hz, 0-100%)
                        + 0x3FA.ACCEL_PEDAL_LOW_RATE (derived/smoothed)
  - BRAKE_PRESSED     = 0x112.BRAKE_PRESSED   (bus 1, 100Hz, 1-bit; low-rate echo at 0x666 byte 5 bit 3)
  - BRAKE_PRESSURE    = 0x224.BRAKE_PRESSURE  (bus 1, ~50Hz, 8-bit 0-255 raw pedal position)
    Analog brake pedal position, validated on 6 driving sessions incl. the
    progressive-brake modulation in 2026-04-17--21-15-57 (t=12-17s window
    shows full 0-255 modulation pattern during a 5s brake press).
  - LONG_ACCEL        = 0x112.LONG_ACCEL      (bus 1, 100Hz, 10-bit, 0.02 m/s^2)
  - TURN_SIGNAL_RIGHT = 0x58B.TURN_SIGNAL_RIGHT (bus 0, 10Hz, byte 11 bit 1)
                        Echo candidate in 0x588 byte 19 bit 1 (corr +0.38, weak)
  - TURN_SIGNAL_LEFT  = 0x112.TURN_SIGNAL_LEFT (bus 1, 100Hz, byte 14 bit 0; DUP at byte 24 bit 0)
                        LEFT is NOT at 0x58B - R5 splits L/R across different messages.
  - LKA_CMD.TORQUE_CMD = 0x134 byte 10 (bus 0, 100Hz, 24B) - validated end-to-end
                        Zero when disengaged; nonzero 3-5% of engaged frames during lane-drift corrections.
                        Bytes 16-17 are SecOC MAC + counter (NOT a control quantity).
  - ACC_SET_SPEED     = 0x5EF.SET_SPEED + SET_SPEED_DUP (bus 0, 1Hz, 8B km/h);
                        also redundantly at 0x3E6.SET_SPEED.
  - ADAS_ENGAGED      = 0x4B5 byte 0 (8-bit multi-valued; 0 = off, 44-58 observed = engaged levels)
  - FOLLOW_DISTANCE   = 0x4D1 byte 3 bits 3-4 (2-bit enum: 0=UNSET, 1=NEAR, 2=MID, 3=FAR)
  - CRUISE_BUTTONS    = 0x4D1 bytes 5-6 (BUTTON_A_RES, B_SPEED, C_SET, D_CANCEL - bit-level)
  - MOTOR_RPM         = 0x67D.MOTOR_RPM       (both buses, 2Hz)

== Signals found in 2026-04-21 engaged session ==
  - ADAS_ENGAGED    = 0x4B5.ADAS_ENGAGED   (byte 0, ALWAYS 0 in unengaged OLD
    session, ALWAYS non-zero in NEW session during sustained ~4min engagement).
    This is the cleanest engagement-mode flag found - a single byte value
    distinguishing "ADAS armed/active" (non-zero) from "ADAS off" (zero).
    Multi-valued (observed 0..58) so decode further into state enum later.
  - LANE_DETECTED   = 0x314.LANE_DETECTED  (byte 57, 0xFF when no lanes seen,
    non-0xFF when camera detects lane markings; bytes 58..63 then carry the
    lane-model payload). Goes active briefly during engagement when lanes
    are clear.
  - ACC_TARGET_PRESENT_A/B = 0x34F.OBJ_A/B_PRESENT (byte 1 / byte 29; 0xFF
    means no target, any other value means target tracked). Only active
    during engagement AND when a lead is detected.
  - 0x34F.OBJ_A/B_UNK_25_26/53_54: renamed from "DISTANCE" - while corr -0.99
    with ego speed in ONE target-tracking window suggested distance, the same
    bytes varied in the OLD (unengaged) session too. Meaning unclear.

== GPS / location data: NOT on either captured bus ==
Searched thoroughly for lat/lon signatures (smooth 16/32-bit bidirectional
fields proportional to speed) with negative result. The R5 likely carries
GPS on its telematics/infotainment bus (not captured here) or via UDS
diagnostics on demand (GDPR-driven). To obtain GPS:
  - Hook red-panda to the telematics CAN (OCU/TBU module - separate from
    the ADAS/chassis buses captured here)
  - Or query UDS service 0x22 ReadDataByIdentifier with proprietary DIDs

== Time/frame counters found (not GPS, but useful) ==
  - Object-cluster messages (0x302, 0x311, ... 0x3DF, 0x34B, etc.) all carry
    a 16-bit time counter at bytes 25-26 and byte 53-54 (mirrored on obj B),
    ticking at ~4.3 Hz regardless of motion. Per-frame monotonic; useful for
    deterministic sequencing of ADAS object data.

Note on false-positive "odometers": 0x496/0x517/0x4D1/0x4B3 were initially
flagged as motion-dependent accumulators because their 32-bit windows showed
large monotonic growth with rate~0 when stopped. On inspection that growth
came entirely from the ECU initialization transition (bytes all-zero before
handshake, settling to meaningful content after a few seconds) - NOT a real
accumulator. These are status/reference messages, not odometers.

CONFIRMED - LKA CMD = 0x134 (validated against 1400+ seconds of engagement
across 3 heavily-engaged drives 2026-04-21):
  byte 10 is nonzero ONLY during engagement (0% of unengaged frames vs 3-5%
  of engaged frames). Matches GWM's BO_ 299 STEER_CMD pattern: small scalar
  cmd + large BYPASS_ME MAC. During sustained straight cruise the cmd is
  ~0; it activates briefly to correct lane drift.
    - byte 10 = 8-bit TORQUE_CMD (zero at idle, nonzero during corrections)
    - bytes 16-17 = 16-bit BYPASSME_SECOC_MAC (76-pair rolling code)
    - byte 17 high nibble = 4-bit COUNTER (SecOC sequence counter)
  Note: 0x134 does NOT follow the standard Renault CRC-at-byte-4 /
  COUNTER-at-byte-5-high convention. Bytes 0-9 and 11-15 are constant -
  the ONLY active payload is byte 10 (cmd) and bytes 16-17 (MAC).

CONFIRMED - ACC SET_SPEED at 0x5EF bytes 3 and 4 (8-bit, km/h):
  Held value 48 during a 47 km/h cruise (2026-04-21--11-46-06 drive); 0 when
  disengaged. Redundant copy at 0x3E6 byte 6. Both are 8-bit unsigned km/h.

STATIONARY CAPTURE (2026-04-22, ~22 min) added the following insights:
  - 0x4B4 byte 3 bit 7 = HEARTBEAT (rapid flip-flop); low 7 bits = real state.
  - 0x588 byte 8 bit 2 is a composite door/warning bit (40% on during Phase-1
    door test, 3.6% during driving). Currently labelled
    DOOR_DRIVER_OPEN_CANDIDATE pending a dedicated per-door test.
  - 0x588 byte 19 bit 1 is the best R-blinker echo candidate (corr +0.38 with
    0x58B.TURN_SIGNAL_RIGHT; 0.04% stationary vs 1.6% driving).
  - LEFT blinker at 0x112.byte14.bit0: validated in 2026-04-21--11-27-00 drive
    (the 2026-04-22 stationary capture didn't trigger LEFT - driver forgot).
  - 0x588 body message has rich byte-level activity 0-200s (Phase 1 doors /
    Phase 2 belts period) but no single bit cleanly maps to per-door state
    from the capture alone - too many co-activating bits. Likely door/belt
    info is encoded as a packed status bitfield, not per-feature bits.
  - PHASE 6 (cruise buttons t=256-472s): placed 4 button bits in 0x4D1 bytes
    5-6 (BUTTON_A_RES/B_SPEED/C_SET/D_CANCEL); FOLLOW_DISTANCE 2-bit enum
    found at 0x4D1 byte 3 bits 3-4 (Nissan ProPilot pattern match).
  - PHASE 5 (gear cycle t=1253-1295s): yielded GEAR_DIRECTION (0x44C, 2-bit),
    REVERSE_LIGHT (0x453), GEAR_SELECTED (0x224), FORWARD_GEAR_ACTIVE (0x112).

MISSING / NOT CONFIRMED (confirmed via 26-session / 19M-frame multi-session sweep):
  - YAW_RATE / LAT_ACCEL: still no clean IMU signal. 0x22A has 4 SPEED_LIKE_*
    fields (corr 0.58-0.72 with speed) but nothing yaw-rate-like
    (r ~ v*steer/wheelbase). A 16-bit signed scan against (speed * d(steer)/dt)
    across all driving sessions produced NO candidate with |corr| > 0.5.
    Likely on ESP/ABS-specific internal bus not captured by the panda.
  - HANDS_ON_WHEEL 1-bit indicator: best R5 candidates correlate only
    0.47-0.55 with |DRIVER_TORQUE|>200. Software must threshold DRIVER_TORQUE.
  - STEER_RATE direction: 0x112 byte 8 is magnitude only. Use 0x1AB.STEER_RATE
    (16-bit signed, scale 0.01 deg/s) for the signed version.
  - Individual doors/belts: 0x588 STATUS bits have composite activity; per-door
    bits not isolable from current captures. Needs dedicated door-by-door
    capture with ≥5s gaps.
  - Battery SOC / 12V voltage / odometer: searched for monotonic growth/decay
    within the longest drive (10 min highway) and across all sessions - no
    clean candidate on the captured buses. Likely on battery/telematics bus.
  - GPS: confirmed NOT on either captured bus.
  - 0x226.VEHICLE_SPEED_A/B are misnamed: raw values reach 29.2 when stopped
    and 60-62 when actual speed is 40 kph (1.5x ratio). Interpretation
    unclear; renamed to UNK_SPEED_LIKE pending cleaner correlation study.

== Undecoded active addresses (seen in 18-20 of 26 sessions) ==
The following 80+ addresses are active on bus 0/1 in nearly every session but
not yet decoded. Most are likely battery BMS (0x5xx cluster), TPMS/diagnostic
(0x6xx cluster), HVAC/infotainment, or redundant status messages that aren't
critical for openpilot lateral control:
  0x192, 0x22B, 0x22C, 0x22D, 0x22E, 0x231, 0x25E, 0x2BA, 0x300, 0x301,
  0x3E1, 0x3EA, 0x422, 0x450, 0x451, 0x490, 0x496, 0x499, 0x4B3, 0x4C5,
  0x4CF, 0x4D2, 0x4EE, 0x4FE, 0x517, 0x528, 0x52B, 0x52C, 0x52D, 0x53B,
  0x53C, 0x541, 0x54C, 0x551, 0x559, 0x562, 0x565, 0x57A, 0x585, 0x587,
  0x593, 0x5A8, 0x5BA, 0x5D1, 0x5DD, 0x5DE, 0x5E6, 0x5EE, 0x611, 0x615,
  0x616, 0x62B, 0x631, 0x665, 0x67A, 0x691, 0x692, 0x6AD, + misc 6xx range.
Extended 29-bit IDs 0x1BDE62F2, 0x1BDEF262 appeared in 2 sessions (likely
FOB or UDS diagnostic responses).
0x422 and 0x496 have CRC+COUNTER+SecOC-MAC-like structure; 0x300 broadcasts
static calibration data (first and last frame identical across 113k frames).

== Non-scalar fields (do NOT treat as signals) ==
  - 0x134.BYPASSME_SECOC_MAC (bytes 16-17): 76-pair rolling code, likely a
    SecOC MAC wrapping the LKA torque command (see BO_ 308 LKA_CMD_134).
    Pattern mirrors GWM Haval H6 STEER_CMD and AUTOSAR SecOC spec.
  - 0x112.BYPASSME_TAIL_*: 80 bits of full-entropy payload at end of frame.
  - 0x355: 58 of 64 bytes are full-entropy; likely CRC-protected proprietary
    camera/radar telemetry.
  - 0x22A has ~16 signed 16-bit fields that go from 'pinned-at-fixed-value'
    when disengaged to 'full signed range' during engagement. Mixture of real
    ADAS data and rolling crypto tokens; NOT a clean cmd channel.";


